A||||2||Master_Sushi||Pre 88 brakes...||||4||
Z||000000||Master_Sushi||11-26-2001||11:41 PM||||Hey, I have a question about pre 88 brakes. As some of you might know I use to own an 88 fiero. It had pretty much mint everything. Now i'm driving an 84se through the winter to sell in the spring.<BR>Anyway, to the point. I have noticed that I can get all four wheels really smoking and smelling bad if I do a quick stop or two from 75-90 (I haven't tried a triple digit yet, I really dont trust my fiero at those speeds).<BR>I was just wondering if this is common/normal, or if my cars brakes are screwed up.<BR>I only smoked the brakes on my 88 once, and this was from multiple high speed stops stops.<P>OH, and do the solid rotors ever warp? I cant stand warped rotors  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">. Will cross drilled fiero store rotors help cooling much? Or will "higher quality" pads do anything?<P>As allways, thanks!||||reg||4
Z||000001||Standard||11-27-2001||12:44 AM||||The smoking/smelling most likely has to do with the pads.  Cross drilling has nothing to do with cooling, and is pretty much worthless.  It was needed when brake pad compounds created a lot of gas under braking.  What could happen with solid rotors then was a thin layer of gas would build up between the pad and rotor, and hamper braking performance.  So cross drilling would give the gas somewhere to go.<P>Yes, solid rotors warp.  The only way to get better cooling is to use vaned rotors,  like the 88's.  Grand Am brake upgrade is one option, but you should never have a problem with warping rotors during normal street driving.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">          <BR><FONT COLOR="red">1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed</FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1"><FONT COLOR="white">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="blue">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fformula88||11-27-2001||12:29 PM||||Did you put the pads in?  Maybe they are cheap/old pads?  All rotors certainly can warp.  I know my 88 has at least one warped rotor, but I have been living with it since its not too bad (yet).  <P>Maybe by doing such aggressive stopping your just getting those brakes too hot!  They are not the ideal setup for spirited driving.  Before going nuts with all kinds of expensive stuff, I'd try some high quality pads.  You might also want to consider going with new calipers or rebuilding them if you buy any expensive pads and/or rotors.  If one goes bad you don't want it messing up the rest of your new hardware!||||reg||1
